some size and pretension, though the name is commonly misapplied, especially in Great Britain, to a cottage, or to one of the class of cheap houses built on speculation in the suburbs of a city; in ''old Eng. law'', a manor.
{{smaller block|A certaine Gentleman called Bassano . . . lived at a ''Villa'' that he had in the country.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 170.}}}}

'''villadom''' (vil′ạ-dum), ''n.'' [< ''villa'' + ''-dom.''] Villas collectively; hence, the persons living in them. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|''Villadom'' of the suburbs votes for the internal divisions of London, and again in the suburban boroughs.
{{right|''Fortnightly Rev.'', N. S., XL. 254.}}}}

'''village''' (vil′ạ̄j), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [< ME. ''village'', < OF. (and F.) ''village'' = Sp. ''villaje'' = Pg. ''villagem'' = It. ''villaggio'', a village, hamlet, < L. ''villaticus'', belonging to a villa or farm-house, < ''villa'', a country house, a farm: see ''vill.'' Cf. ''villatic.''] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A small assemblage of houses, less than a town or city, and larger than a hamlet. {{smaller|In many of the United States the incorporated village exists as the least populous kind of corporate municipality. Its boundaries are usually not identical with those of any primary division of the county, but include only the space occupied by houses adjoining or nearly adjoining.}}
{{smaller block|The same daye we passyd Pauya, and lay y<sup>t</sup> nyght at Seint Jacobo, a ''vyllage.''
{{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 5.}}
A walled town is more worthier than a ''village.''
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, iii. 3. 60.}}
I resolved to go forward until I could discover some house or ''village.''
{{right|''Swift'', Gulliver’s Travels, iv. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''law'', sometimes a manor; sometimes a

readings.c1s2:
whole parish or subdivision of it; most commonly an outpart of a parish, consisting of a few houses separate from the rest.—'''Prairie-dog village.''' {{smaller|See ''prairie-dog''.}} ='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|''Hamlet'', etc. See ''town''.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Of, pertaining to, or belonging to a village; characteristic of a village; hence, rustic; countrified.
{{smaller block|<poem>The early ''village'' cock
Hath twice done salutation to the morn.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., v. 3. 209.}}
<poem>Some ''village'' Hampden, that, with dauntless breast,
The little tyrant of his fields withstood. ''Gray'', Elegy.</poem>}}
'''Village cart.''' {{smaller|See ''cart''.}}—'''Village community.''' {{smaller|See ''community''. See also ''manor'', ''villeinage''. For the village community in Russia, see ''mir''.}}—'''Village mark.''' {{smaller|See ''mark''<sup>1</sup>, 14.}}

'''village-moot''' (vil′ạ̄j-möt), ''n.'' In ''early Eng. hist.'', the assembly of the men of a village. See ''moot''<sup>1</sup>.

'''villager''' (vil′ạ̄j-ėr), ''n.'' [< ''village'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] An inhabitant of a village.
{{smaller block|<poem>Brutus had rather be a ''villager''
Than to repute himself a son of Rome
Under these hard conditions.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., i. 2. 172.}}}}

'''villagery†''' (vil′ạ̄j-ri), ''n.'' [< ''village'' + ''-(e)ry''.] A group of villages.
{{smaller block|The maidens of the ''villagery''. ''Shak.'', M. N. D., ii. 1. 35.}}

'''villain''' (vil′ạ̄n), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [Also archaically, in legal and historical use, ''villein;'' formerly sometimes ''villan'', early mod. E. ''vilayn'', etc.; < ME. ''vilain'', ''vilein'', ''vileyn'', also sometimes ''vilains'', ''vilans'', ''vileyns'', < OF. ''vilein'', ''vilain'', ''villain'', ''villein'', nom. also ''vilains'', ''vilainz'', F. ''vilain'', a farm-ser-

readings.c1s3:
vant, serf, peasant, clown, scoundrel, also adj. base, mean, wicked, = Pr. ''vilan'', ''vila'' = Sp. ''villano'' = Pg. ''villão'' = It. ''villano'', < ML. ''villanus'', a farm-servant, serf, clown, < L. ''villa'', a farm: see ''vill''. The forms ''villain'', ''villein'', etc., are historically one, and the attempt to differentiate them in meaning is idle.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A member of the lowest class of unfree persons during the prevalence of the feudal system; a feudal serf.
{{smaller block|In respect to their lords or owners the villains had no rights, except that the lord might not kill or maim them, or ravish the females; they could acquire or hold no property against their lord’s will; they were obliged to perform all the menial services he demanded; and the cottages and plots of land they occupied were held merely at his will. In respect, however, of other persons besides their lord they had the rights and privileges of freemen. Villains were either ''regardant'' (which see) or ''in gross''. They were in view of the law annexed to the soil (''adscripti'' or ''adscriptitii glebæ''), belonging to a manor as fixtures, passing with it when it was conveyed or inherited, and they could not be sold or transferred as persons separate from the land. The latter belonged personally to their lord, who could sell or transfer them at will.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Villain?'' by my blood,
I am as free-born as your Venice duke!</poem>
{{right|''Middleton'', Blurt, Master-Constable, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|The ''villeins'' owe to the lord all sorts of dues and services, personal labour, among others, on the lands which form his domain; they may not leave the Manor without his permission; no one of them can succeed to the land of another without his assent; and the legal theory even is that the movable property of the ''villein'' belongs to the lord. Yet it may confidently be laid down that, in the light of modern research, none of these disadvantages}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|prove an absolutely servile status, and that all may be explained without reference to it.
{{right|''Maine'', Early Law and Custom, p. 305.}}
The ''villain'' was not a slave, but a freeman minus the very important rights of his lord.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Norman Conquest, V. 320.}}}}

Hence—'''2.''' An ignoble or base-born person generally; a boor, peasant, or clown.
{{smaller block|Pour the blood of the ''villain'' in one basin, and the blood of the gentleman in another, what difference shall here be proved? {{right|''Bacon.''}}
''May.'' Where is your mistress, ''villain?'' when went she abroad?
''Pren.'' Abroad, sir! why, as soon as she was up, sir.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, i. 3.}}}}

'''3.''' A man of ignoble or base character; especially, one who is guilty or capable of gross wickedness; a scoundrel; a knave; a rascal; a rogue: often used humorously in affectionate or jocose reproach.
{{smaller block|<poem>One may smile, and smile, and be a ''villain.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 5. 108.}}
<poem>This ring is mine; he was a ''villain''
That stole it from my hand; he was a ''villain''
That put it into yours.</poem>
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, i. 3.}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Of or pertaining to, or consisting of, villains or serfs.
{{smaller block|The ''villein'' class, notwithstanding legal and canonical hindrances, aspired to holy orders as one of the avenues to liberty. {{right|''Stubbs'', Const. Hist., § 405.}}}}

'''2.''' Characteristic of or befitting a villain or slave; servile; base; villainous.
{{smaller block|For thou art the moste ''vileyn'' knyght that euer I mette in my lif. {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 690.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|Ille happe haue he, that ''vylenis'' [read ''vyleins''?] knyght, that asketh eny tribute of eny trauellynge knyghtes.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 127.}}
''Vileyns'' sinful dedes make a cherl.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Wife of Bath’s Tale, l. 302.}}
''Villain'' bonds and despot sway. ''Byron'', Giaour.}}

'''Villein services''', in ''feudal law'', base or menial services performed in consideration of the tenure of land.

{{smaller block|The records of ''villein services'' will be jealously scanned in the present state of the controversy on the question of the village community. ''Athenæum'', No. 3141, p. 11.}}

'''Villein socage.''' {{smaller|See ''socage''.}}

'''villain†''' (vil′ạn), ''v. t.'' [Early mod. E. also ''vilayn''; < ''villain'', ''n.''] To debase; degrade; villainize.

{{smaller block|When they haue once ''vilayned'' the sacrament of matrimonye. {{right|''Sir T. More'', Works, p. 344.}}}}

'''villainage''' (vil′ạn-āj), ''n.'' [< ''villain'' + ''-age''. Cf. ''villeinage''.] The condition of a villain or peasant.

{{smaller block|While the churl sank to the state of ''villainage'', the slave rose to it. ''E. A. Freeman'', Norman Conquest, V. 322.}}

'''villainize†''' (vil′ạn-īz), ''v. t.'' [Also ''villanize''; < ''villain'' + ''-ize''.] To debase; degrade; defame; revile; calumniate.

{{smaller block|<poem>Were virtue by descent, a noble name
Could never ''villanize'' his father’s fame.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Wife of Bath’s Tale, l. 405.}}}}

'''villainizer†''' (vil′ạn-ī-zėr), ''n.'' [Also ''villanizer''; < ''villainize'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who villainizes.

'''villainly†''', ''adv.'' [ME. ''vileynsly''; < ''villain'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Wretchedly; wickedly; villainously.

{{smaller block|And there was oure Lord first scourged; for he was scourged and ''vileynsly'' entreted in many places.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 95.}}}}

